24:T1347,<h2>Overview</h2>
<p>People often expect depression to feel like crying. More often it feels like nothing: food without taste, music without pull, a to-do list that may as well be in another language.</p>
<p>That absence is a symptom, not a personality. Depression narrows the brain’s reward signalling, so activities that used to pay off no longer register — which makes withdrawal feel rational and deepens the loop.</p>
<h2>Why willpower is the wrong lever</h2>
<p>In depression, the anticipation of reward dims before the capacity for it does. You can still enjoy a walk; you simply cannot feel in advance that you would. Waiting to feel like it means waiting for the last symptom to lift first.</p>
<p>This is why behavioural activation works: small scheduled actions taken without motivation slowly restore the reward signal. Mood follows movement rather than leading it.</p>
<h2>Signs &amp; Patterns to Notice</h2>
<p>These are common patterns and bodily stress responses rather than a formal diagnosis:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>[Mind] Loss of interest:</strong> Things that reliably mattered — people, work, food — go flat. Clinicians call this anhedonia.</li>
<li><strong>[Body] Leaden fatigue:</strong> Sleep does not restore. Ordinary tasks cost several times their usual energy.</li>
<li><strong>[Mind] Harsh self-narration:</strong> A running commentary of burden, failure, and worthlessness that feels factual.</li>
<li><strong>[Behavior] Withdrawal from contact:</strong> Cancelling, leaving messages unread, shrinking the day down to survival.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What People Believe vs. What Evidence Shows</h2>

        <blockquote>
          <p><strong>Myth:</strong> “Depression is caused by a simple chemical imbalance.”</p>
          <p><strong>Fact:</strong> It involves biology, stress, sleep, circumstance, and thought patterns together. Treatment works on several of those at once.</p>
        </blockquote>
      

        <blockquote>
          <p><strong>Myth:</strong> “If you can work and smile, it is not real depression.”</p>
          <p><strong>Fact:</strong> High-functioning depression is common. Capability at work says nothing about internal cost.</p>
        </blockquote>
      

        <blockquote>
          <p><strong>Myth:</strong> “You have to want to get better first.”</p>
          <p><strong>Fact:</strong> Ambivalence is a symptom. Treatment can begin while hope is still absent.</p>
        </blockquote>
      
<h2>Practical Coping Strategies</h2>
<h3>Behavioural activation ladder</h3>
<p>Builds momentum from actions small enough to be undeniable.</p>
<ol>
<li>List activities that used to matter, ranked by effort.</li>
<li>Schedule the two easiest at fixed times tomorrow.</li>
<li>Do them at the scheduled time regardless of mood.</li>
<li>Rate mood before and after — collect evidence, not opinions.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The two-minute floor</h3>
<p>Removes the negotiation about whether today is a good enough day.</p>
<ol>
<li>Choose one anchor task: teeth, shower, one glass of water, one open window.</li>
<li>Commit to two minutes, with permission to stop after.</li>
<li>Do it at the same time daily so it stops requiring a decision.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Thought-record reframing</h3>
<p>Separates a feeling from a verdict about who you are.</p>
<ol>
<li>Write the thought verbatim, including the cruelty.</li>
<li>Note the evidence for it, then the evidence against it.</li>
<li>Write a version you would say to someone you respect.</li>
</ol>
<h2>How to Support Someone Nearby</h2>
<h3>Helpful phrases to say:</h3>
<ul>
<li>““I am bringing dinner Thursday at seven. You do not have to talk.””</li>
<li>““You are not a burden to me. I am choosing to be here.””</li>
<li>““Would it help if I made the appointment call with you?””</li>
</ul>
<h3>Phrases to avoid:</h3>
<ul>
<li>““You have so much to be grateful for.””</li>
<li>““Have you tried exercising more?””</li>
<li>““Snap out of it — this isn’t you.””</li>
</ul>
<h2>Common Questions</h2>

        <div style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
          <p><strong>Should I push someone to socialise?</strong></p>
          <p>Invite gently and repeatedly without penalty for declining. Pressure adds guilt; a standing, no-obligation invitation keeps the door open.</p>
        </div>
      

        <div style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
          <p><strong>When is it urgent?</strong></p>
          <p>If someone mentions wanting to die, giving possessions away, or has a plan, treat it as urgent. Stay with them and call or text 988 together.</p>
        </div>
      

        <div style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
          <p><strong>Can depression lift on its own?</strong></p>
          <p>Some episodes remit without treatment, but untreated episodes tend to be longer and more likely to recur. Treatment shortens and softens them.</p>
        </div>
